# I. Values and Ethics, IA. Ethical principles and responsibilities (24)
CAT = "aswbbachEthics"; SEG = "ETH"
Q = [
("A social worker gives some hours each month, unpaid, to a free clinic. Which NASW core value does this MOST reflect?",
 "Service", ["Integrity", "Competence", "Social justice"],
 "Under the value of service, the NASW Code encourages social workers to volunteer some of their professional skills with no expectation of significant financial return. Social justice is about challenging unjust conditions, not giving one's own time."),
("A client signed a consent form but was never told she could stop services at any time. Her consent is missing:",
 "Her right to withdraw", ["A witness signature", "A notarized copy", "Her doctor's approval"],
 "Informed consent covers the purpose of services, risks, costs, alternatives and the client's right to refuse or withdraw consent. A witness or a notary does not make consent informed."),
("A case manager wants to look up a new client online before the first home visit. She should FIRST:",
 "Get the client's consent", ["Search public pages only", "Tell her supervisor afterward", "Delete anything she finds"],
 "The NASW Code says social workers should obtain client consent before conducting an electronic search on the client, unless there is a compelling professional reason. Limiting the search to public pages does not remove that duty."),
("A social worker must disclose a client's threat against a named coworker. When feasible, she should also:",
 "Tell him before she discloses", ["Wait until he leaves the building", "Ask him to sign a release", "Keep the disclosure off the record"],
 "The Code asks social workers to inform clients, to the extent possible, about a disclosure and its likely consequences before it is made. A release is not needed when disclosure is required to prevent serious harm."),
("Which of these is protected health information under HIPAA?",
 "Name and diagnosis on a bill", ["Data stripped of identifiers", "Staff files kept as employer", "A diary kept at home"],
 "PHI is individually identifiable health information held by a covered entity, such as a name linked to a diagnosis on a bill. De-identified data and employment records the agency holds as an employer are excluded."),
("In common ethical decision-making models, which step comes LAST?",
 "Monitor and review the result", ["List everyone the choice affects", "Weigh each option against the code", "Consult with trusted colleagues"],
 "Models such as Reamer's end by monitoring, evaluating and documenting the decision after it is carried out. Identifying who is affected, weighing options and consulting all come before the decision."),
("A social worker involves a client's family and neighbors as partners in helping. Which NASW value does this MOST reflect?",
 "Importance of human relationships", ["Dignity and worth of the person", "Social and economic justice", "Integrity in professional work"],
 "The value of the importance of human relationships asks social workers to engage people as partners in the helping process and strengthen relationships among them. Dignity and worth centers on respect for each individual."),
("The sense of fulfillment a social worker gets from helping clients well is called:",
 "Compassion satisfaction", ["Countertransference", "Emotional contagion", "Vicarious resilience"],
 "Compassion satisfaction is the positive side of helping work and offsets compassion fatigue. Moral residue is the lingering distress after being unable to act on one's values."),
("In-service training is BEST described as:",
 "Training the employer gives on the job", ["Graduate coursework for a degree", "A hearing before the licensing board", "Supervision toward a new license"],
 "In-service training is professional development provided within the agency, often on new policies or skills. The outline lists it with workshops, literature reviews and supervision as ways to stay current."),
("A field instructor wants to date the BSW student she supervises. Under the NASW Code this is:",
 "Not allowed for her", ["Allowed if the student starts it", "Allowed if the school is told", "Allowed if they meet off site"],
 "The Code says social workers who function as supervisors or educators should not engage in sexual activities with supervisees, students or trainees over whom they exercise professional authority. Who starts it or where they meet does not change that."),
("A social worker discusses a client by name with a coworker in a crowded hospital elevator. This MOST violates:",
 "Confidentiality", ["Informed consent", "Self-determination", "Privileged communication"],
 "The Code says social workers should not discuss confidential information in public or semipublic areas such as hallways, waiting rooms and elevators. Privilege is a legal protection from being compelled to testify, not the duty broken here."),
("A laptop holding client records is stolen from a social worker's car. Under the NASW Code, she should:",
 "Notify the affected clients", ["Notify only clients with open cases", "Report it to police and stop there", "Notify clients once misuse is proven"],
 "The Code asks social workers to follow policies on notifying clients of unauthorized access to their records, including breaches of electronic records, consistent with applicable laws. Waiting for misuse leaves clients unprotected."),
("A social worker presents a client's case at a conference for teaching. She should:",
 "Disguise identifying details", ["Use the client's first name only", "Ask the audience to keep quiet", "Present only cases now closed"],
 "When using client information for teaching, the Code calls for removing identifying information unless the client has consented or there is a compelling need. A first name and a closed case can still identify someone."),
("A reporter calls a social worker to ask whether a local man is her client. She should:",
 "Neither confirm nor deny it", ["Confirm only that he is a client", "Refer the reporter to his family", "Share only general facts about him"],
 "Even the fact that someone is a client is confidential. The Code asks social workers to protect confidentiality when responding to requests from the media."),
("A client asks her agency to correct an error in her health record. Under HIPAA, she has the right to:",
 "Request an amendment", ["Have the page destroyed", "Rewrite the note herself", "Remove the provider's notes"],
 "HIPAA gives individuals the right to request an amendment to their record. The provider may accept or deny the request in writing; records are corrected or added to, not destroyed or rewritten by the client."),
("Under HIPAA, the written explanation of how an agency may use and share client information is the:",
 "Notice of privacy practices", ["Release of information form", "Business associate agreement", "Consent to treatment form"],
 "Covered entities must give clients a notice of privacy practices describing uses, disclosures and their rights. A release of information authorizes one specific disclosure."),
("In a case note a social worker calls a client 'a manipulative drunk.' This conflicts with the duty to use:",
 "Respectful, accurate wording", ["Person-first diagnostic codes", "Plain-language summaries", "Exact times for every contact"],
 "The Code says social workers should use accurate and respectful language in all communications to and about clients. The note should describe observed behavior instead."),
("After a tornado, a shelter asks local social workers for help. The NASW Code says social workers should:",
 "Help to the greatest extent possible", ["Leave it to disaster agencies", "Wait for agency approval first", "Help only clients she already serves"],
 "Under ethical responsibilities to society, social workers should provide appropriate professional services in public emergencies to the greatest extent possible. The duty is not limited to existing clients."),
("An adult client with advanced dementia cannot consent to a move. The social worker should seek consent from:",
 "Her legal decision-maker", ["The nursing home administrator", "The agency's medical director", "Her closest neighbor"],
 "When clients lack the capacity to give informed consent, the Code says social workers should seek permission from an appropriate third party, such as a guardian or health care agent, and still inform the client in a way she can understand."),
("A court orders a parent to attend services. At intake, the social worker should explain:",
 "What is involved and refusal limits", ["That services are fully optional", "Only the court's reporting rules", "That records stay private from court"],
 "For clients receiving services involuntarily, the Code asks social workers to explain the nature and extent of services and the extent of the client's right to refuse. Telling a court-ordered parent that refusal has no consequences would be untrue."),
("According to the NASW Code, which core value ranks highest?",
 "None; it sets no ranking", ["Service, then social justice", "Social justice, above all others", "Integrity, then competence"],
 "The Code says it does not specify which values, principles and standards are most important when they conflict. Social workers use professional judgment and consultation to weigh them."),
("A social worker reads current studies before choosing a parenting curriculum for her agency. This BEST shows:",
 "Evidence-informed practice", ["Cultural humility", "Professional advocacy", "Client self-determination"],
 "Reviewing research to choose interventions is evidence-informed practice, one of the professional development activities the outline lists. Cultural humility is ongoing self-reflection about culture."),
("Which agency condition MOST raises the risk of staff burnout?",
 "Heavy caseloads, little support", ["Weekly group supervision sessions", "Flexible work schedules for staff", "Clear written job descriptions"],
 "Burnout grows from chronic overload, low control and little support. Supervision, flexibility and clear roles are protective."),
("When giving a client a copy of her file, the social worker should protect the confidentiality of:",
 "Other people named in it", ["The client's own diagnosis", "The agency's billing codes", "The intake worker's name"],
 "The Code says that when providing clients with access to their records, social workers should protect the confidentiality of other individuals identified or discussed in the records. The client's own diagnosis is hers to see."),
]
